# The installers are fetched raw and piped straight into a shell, so a CRLF
# in install.sh means "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" on every Linux box. Pin
# the line endings regardless of anyone's core.autocrlf setting.
install.sh text eol=lf
uninstall.sh text eol=lf
install.ps1 text eol=crlf
uninstall.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Fonts are binary. core.autocrlf is on here, and a line-ending "fix" applied
# to a TTF corrupts it -- whose symptom is exactly the row of boxes the fonts
# were added to get rid of. Do not leave this to git's content heuristic.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
# Same reasoning for raster assets: a launcher icon that git
# decided was text would be corrupt on checkout.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
